    {
      "id": "s2",
      "discipline": "GEOGRAPHY",
      "title": "Why the water matters",
      "order": 2,
      "state": "READY",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "s2.b1",
          "kind": "prose",
          "version": 1,
          "reuse": {
            "pieceId": "7d4e2c10-5a6b-4c7d-9e8f-0a1b2c3d4e5f",
            "storedAt": "2026-11-12T08:15:00Z"
          },
          "sentences": [
            {
              "id": "s2.b1.1",
              "text": "Presence of iron in the water supply dulls and darkens the final result; alum, tin or chrome deposits help brighten colours.",
              "level": "PROVISIONAL",
              "citations": [
                {
                  "id": "bf7fda76-2629-5609-ac54-d4c011fbb573",
                  "claimId": "826c1d72-9373-59f0-85c3-6bb38aa89320",
                  "sourceId": "d0f53ee9-4b97-5df7-a367-686957bdb7c8",
                  "quote": "Water enriched with alum, tin or chrome mineral deposits will help brighten the colors but presence of iron in the water supply dulls and darkens the final result.",
                  "locator": "GI Journal No. 188 copy (application 1050), PDF p. 8",
                  "context": null,
                  "evidence": "QUOTED",
                  "level": "PROVISIONAL"
                }
              ],
              "translated": false,
              "terms": [
                {
                  "text": "alum",
                  "termKey": "alum"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "s2.b1.2",
              "text": "Ajrakh printing got concentrated where extra soft water was available.",
              "level": "PROVISIONAL",
              "citations": [
                {
                  "id": "c333691c-bb17-5c97-a725-c037ff9f24ce",
                  "claimId": "6d42a538-0a5e-5cfb-b8f5-68012eb0d4d6",
                  "sourceId": "d0f53ee9-4b97-5df7-a367-686957bdb7c8",
                  "quote": "The district gazetteers of 1971 have mentioned that Ajrakh Printing-Ajrakh is another popular variety in hand-printing of textiles in Kutch. Ajrakh printing originally came from Sindh and got concentrated where extra soft water was available.",
                  "locator": "GI Journal No. 188 copy (application 1050), PDF p. 3",
                  "context": null,
                  "evidence": "QUOTED",
                  "level": "PROVISIONAL"
                }
              ],
              "translated": false,
              "terms": [
                {
                  "text": "soft water",
                  "termKey": "soft-water"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "s2.b1.3",
              "text": "Excess iron in groundwater threatened Ajrakh in Dhamadka and is again threatening it in Ajrakhpur.",
              "level": "PROVISIONAL",
              "citations": [
                {
                  "id": "02b489ed-7b2a-5ece-bb34-caf590e4b31f",
                  "claimId": "faa466c1-8940-5552-a7f5-851cebb40f64",
                  "sourceId": "907e2c52-14e7-5e41-9b27-9c10f70906a9",
                  "quote": "Excess iron in groundwater that threatened Ajrakh in Dhamadka, is once again threatening the craft in Ajrakhpur.",
                  "locator": "§ \"Share this article\"",
                  "context": null,
                  "evidence": "QUOTED",
                  "level": "PROVISIONAL"
                }
              ],
              "translated": false,
              "terms": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "searched": null
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "discipline": "CHEMISTRY",
      "title": "The chemistry of the colours",
      "order": 3,
      "state": "READY",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "s3.b1",
          "kind": "prose",
          "version": 1,
          "reuse": null,
          "sentences": [
            {
              "id": "s3.b1.1",
              "text": "Ajrakh colours come from indigo, madder and iron acetate.",
              "level": "PROVISIONAL",
              "citations": [
                {
                  "id": "f5e2ff39-d761-5d50-bcff-34138c663abe",
                  "claimId": "feaa25b3-b79c-59ce-9c6b-1a841c7e12ed",
                  "sourceId": "4ce205e3-653f-51db-9e49-14fb3b3f2e14",
                  "quote": "1 The colors are created with a complex process of resist and mordant printing, and dyeing with indigo, madder, and iron acetate (Figure 3).",
                  "locator": "§ \"Indigo in Kutch, India: Art and Science, Environment and Economics\"",
                  "context": null,
                  "evidence": "QUOTED",
                  "level": "PROVISIONAL"
                }
              ],
              "translated": false,
              "terms": [
                {
                  "text": "indigo",
                  "termKey": "indigo"
                },
                {
                  "text": "madder",
                  "termKey": "madder"
                },
                {
                  "text": "iron acetate",
                  "termKey": "iron-acetate"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "s3.b1.2",
              "text": "At first bright green, the cloth rapidly becomes blue as oxygen meets the dye on the fibres.",
              "level": "PROVISIONAL",
              "citations": [
                {
